## D38 · 2026-10-20 · The views database rebuilt: a view is a question with a five-rung ladder and a due date

- By: Alex, over the rebuild discussion logged in `docs/views-rebuild-log.md` (steps 1–13). On the unit: "这个 hypo 更像是一个
  thesis with a state"; on rungs: "我们用 5 个 state 吧 very bullish, bullish, neutral, bearish, very bearish"; on kinds: "有些事情不一定对所有人都是
  bull / bear，有可能完全相反！……这个需要明确分开来看"; on one layer: "一条观点一定是有一个明确的 ladder 和到期日，这样才构成一个观点呀";
  on periods: "这个肯定要写清楚日期", then yes to one view per year (this year and next); on chains: "是不是用 linked list 来存储 lineage 就好";
  on the reading rule and the remaining fixes: "looks right".
- Decides:
  - A view is one question, a ladder of five fixed rungs and a due date, never rewritten. It looks at one number (which
    year, whether a period amount or a point-in-time value, and the source it is answered from) or the state of one
    thing (scope and stage). An object view's identity is its object plus what it looks at; a theme view's identity is
    only what it looks at (the theme is where it is filed), so theme views are compared across all themes. A view looks
    at one thing: a question that needs a number and an event is two views joined by a dependency; a question whose
    parts can move in opposite directions is two views, joined only if one really depends on the other.
  - Object views (companies, crypto, commodities) have the rungs strongly bullish to strongly bearish, meaning good or
    bad for the business, fixed at creation. Theme views have five outcomes on one axis, named by outcome, with no
    bull or bear; each dependent object view's arrow says which end is bullish for it. The stance on the price is our
    state against the consensus, which is not designed yet.
  - Each rung is an observable yes-or-no condition; rungs do not overlap, cover every outcome, and exactly one holds at
    the due date; there is no "partial". A number ladder is ranges of one number in its stated year, judged by code;
    an event ladder is the states one thing can be in on the due date, judged by the model from "happened" evidence
    only, citing it. The model sets the rungs when the view is created; they do not change before the due date.
  - The due date is when the debate is settled, usually 6–12 months out; a number's due date is the day it is
    published; quarterly reports in between are evidence. Number views carry their year (fiscal year for companies,
    calendar year otherwise) and at most this year's and next year's are open at once. The year counts from the latest
    publication day among the items processed so far, which only moves forward (never the wall clock); a year whose
    period has ended gets no new view. An event view is one open view per thing. (Claude's reading of "this year", to be
    confirmed by Alex.)
  - Two positions on a ladder: where it is now (a fact; only "happened" evidence moves it; a point-in-time number takes
    the latest published value, a period amount has none before it is published) and the state (where we think it
    lands; re-judged by the model each time from all of the view's evidence and its dependencies' current state, never
    stepped from the last state; citations checked by code; temperature set in Config). A replay reads the stored
    judgements; it does not call the model again.
  - Evidence is a sentence or number, stored once, with its source and tier (happened, said by a party, third-party
    view, rumour); independent sources are counted by original source; the direction it pushes is written on the link
    between evidence and view by each re-judgement.
  - A dependency B → A carries one direction (which end of A is bullish for B; between themes, same or opposite). A
    relation that looks good-then-bad means a middle view is missing. Notices pass, evidence never does; code marks
    B's evidence that is also linked to A. A number dependency is pinned to its year (a closed A is read by its answer;
    an A not yet created is waited for and connected when created; an unanswerable A is a missing input, never
    neutral); an event dependency follows A's chain. A new view may depend on theme views (found by search) or on
    another object's views (supply chain). B is told when A changes rung, when what B reads changes, or when A cannot
    be answered. No cycles, checked on the graph each
    round reads. A new theme view that succeeds nothing is checked against every open view for missing arrows.
  - Succession: the next view on the same thing succeeds the last one, forming a chain that holds the lineage; no
    forks; the successor keeps the rung orientation; a wrong succession is revoked by an appended record; a successor
    proposes its predecessor's dependencies one year on.
  - A view ends answered (the first published value from its stated source; the score compares it with the last state
    committed before the item carrying the answer was processed), unanswerable (with its reason, not scored; a
    processing failure is retried, not unanswerable) or merged. A view whose object leaves the universe is closed as
    unanswerable for now; whether it is scored is open.
    A number view past its period and waiting for its answer does not count against the two open years.
- Replaces: D26's structure (object → driver → view with a claim, direction, horizon class, confirm and falsify
  conditions and a premise); D30's add-only conditions, conflict trigger and consensus-by-code (open again); D31's
  premise links with a seen version.
- Enforced: `VIEW_TREE`, `VIEW_KINDS`, `KIND_NOTE`, `LADDER_EXAMPLES`, `LADDER_RULES`, `POSITIONS`, `TIERS`, `TIER_RULES`,
  `DEPENDENCY_TREE`, `DEPENDENCY_RULES`, `CHAIN_RULES`, `LIFECYCLE_TREE`, `LIFECYCLE_NOTES`, `VIEW_FIELDS`, `EVIDENCE_FIELDS`
  of `docs/system-design/new-facts.js`; the views node of `docs/system-design/new-design.js`; `docs/prd/06-views.md`.
- Changed by D42: a yes-or-no question has two rungs; a re-judgement reads the view's evidence that has not been
  replaced, and a round that brought only repeats and no dependency change does not re-judge; "replaced" also covers a
  later statement by the same speaker and an official confirmation or denial of a rumour; a dependency links only an
  input (B's answer uses A's answer), and a cycle is rewritten.
- Changed by D45: what stands is what points to Alex's words or an earlier decision: a view as a question, a
  ladder of five rungs (two for yes or no) and a due date where the debate resolves; object and theme views; the
  state judged by the model from all evidence not replaced and from the dependencies; evidence stored once with its
  push on the link; tiers (D30); dependencies with arrows read by kind; succession as a chain; this year's and next
  year's number. Where it is now, how rungs are written and judged, answer sources, how the years are counted, the
  details of log step 13, unanswered and waiting views, and how answers are scored are proposals (D45).
- Changed by D51: B is re-judged when what it reads changed (its evidence, or a dependency's conclusion marked
  changed), not only when A changes rung.
## D397 · 2026-11-10 · A topic that asks more than one thing gets one judged question; the other parts are shown as not judged

- **Asked**, after the review council on the Step 4 design (round log §69; two blind readers found 12 to 16 of the 34 opened theses
  asking more than one thing): "新系统自己开的 34 道题里，有 12–16 道一题问了两件事（例如：英伟达会不会给 OpenAI 的俄亥俄数据中心签担保——金额多少？）。给这种题写答案分段时，怎么处理？"
  - Options:
    - judge only the main thing and show the rest as not judged (recommended). The option stated its costs: those parts have no
      answer for now; three memory topics with no main part get no answer; and it departs from his 2026-10-20 rule that a question
      looks at one thing and two things are two questions (D38), recorded as a gap to fill;
    - split into two questions, each judged, as D38 says;
    - do not judge such a topic until Step 1 opens it as one thing.
  - Alex: "只判主要那件 (Recommended)".
- **Decided:**
  - In the clean Step 4's ladder step, a thesis gets at most one judged question: the part its `outcome` names. Every other part of
    its wording is listed and shown beside the answer as not judged.
  - A thesis with no main part gets no judged question, with the reason recorded.
  - For a topic that holds two real bets this departs from D38 until splitting is built. It is an open defect, sized by the first
    test: readers mark each part left out as a bet an analyst would hold or not.
- **Scope:** the clean path's ladder step only. D38 stands for production.

## D436 · 2026-11-13 · One bet seen from two sides is one question; change 3 is dropped; G1-a and G1-b run without it

- **Asked**, after two blind source readers agreed on none of six cases (`clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate1/PREREAD-RESULT.md`):
  "例子：同一篇文章里有'2027 年 HBM 出货增长多少'和'2027 年 HBM 是否仍然供不应求'。两位读者对照原文判：一位说这是两个能分别押注的题，另一位说是同一件事的两面，放一张卡就够。…你怎么看：这类情况应该算一个题还是两个题？"
  - Options: one question, change 3 dropped (recommended): "第二件事作为同一题的背景留着（后面判断时显示为'不判'），第三处改动作废。理由：都是同一个押注的不同侧面；我们的原则是偏谨慎、不要把一件事拆成碎题。"; two questions, change 3 continues; case by case.
  - Alex: "算一个题，第三处不改 (Recommended)".
- **Asked:** "第三处停下后，这两处的小测试（42 次发送，约 0.6 美元，上限 2 美元）要不要照常跑？" Options: run (recommended): "这两处不依赖第三处。任何一项不过就停，报给你。"; wait. Alex: "照常跑 (Recommended)".
- **Decided:** a second side of the same bet (the shortage beside the shipments, the AI-chip share beside the chip total, the capacity keeping pace beside the demand) is one question; it stays as context in that question and is shown as not judged (D397). Change 3 (the `outcome` definition) is dropped, and the "two bets in one thesis" class is closed as not a Step 1 defect under this ruling. G1-a and G1-b run as fixed in `COMBINED-PLAN.md`; the candidate for later gates is c8 with changes 1 and 2 only.
- **Scope:** clean Step 1; c8 stays in force.


DESIGN HEADINGS
# The period split, design v4 (D615; v1 to v3 refused in council rounds 155 to 157), 2026-11-16, $0
## 0. What changed from v1 (round 155)
## 0b. What changed from v2 (round 156)
## 0c. What changed from v3 (round 157); this section governs where earlier sections differ
## 1. The unit, ownership and the writer path
## 2. The boundary and every outcome
## 3. The measurement (a product rule, apart from the official gate)
## 4. The stop condition

DESIGN TEXT
## 0b. What changed from v2 (round 156)

1. **Every yearly attempt has its own identity.** `clean_ladders` gains a non-null `period` column, and its key becomes (`thesis_id`,
   `period`):
   - an unsplit thesis keeps one row with `period = ''`, as today's rows are read;
   - a split parent has its `''` row with status `split` and the two years in its answer;
   - each yearly attempt has a row with `period = 'Y'`, whatever its outcome, and its view or none.
   - **Every reader is specified:**
     - `bundle.ladder_row` reads by `view_id`, unchanged, since a yearly view's row is unique;
     - `stage_b/control_theses.py`, which reads one row per thesis, reads the `''` row. On status `split`, it reads the yearly rows and
       treats each written yearly view as its own candidate for comparison with the ruler's question of that year, and an unwritten
       year as having no view;
     - the writer (`ladder_adapter.open_thesis`) writes the rows.
     - A search of `clean_ladders` over `src/`, `scripts/` and `clean-arch/` finds no other code reader.
2. **"Not judged" depends on the result:**
   - **both years written:** neither view lists the other year;
   - **one written (2026), the other refused:** the 2026 view lists "calendar 2027: no question written (<the refusal's reason>)" as
     not judged;
   - **neither written:** no view, and both refusals recorded on their rows.
   - The list is fixed when the views are written, in the same article, and the bundle and page tests cover all three states.
   - **Yearly view ids (D619):** `v`, 0, the topic's number and the four-digit year (`v042026`): digits only, as the frozen judge
     reads `v\d+`, and never equal to an ordinary id, because the writer refuses a topic id that is not `clean-` and a number without
     a leading zero (Step 1's counter; every stored id has that form). Readers that map a view to its topic by id refuse a split store
     (BUILD-RESULT.md, "Round 160").
   - **Who decides (D618):** whether a part is the other year's quantity is meaning, so the model is told in the yearly line ("{other}
     is asked as its own question, so do not list it as a part this question leaves out"). Code passes the model's parts unchanged
     and adds only the year that has no question. The probe's reading measures whether the model obeys.
3. **The transaction and recovery boundary, from the code:**
   - **The model calls** (the parent's, then each year's) run in the answer-choice step's runner. Each pass has its own archive, and
     the thesis's outcome line is written to `outcomes.jsonl` only after both yearly calls have returned.
   - The runner refuses an existing folder, and a stopped run is never started again (`stage_a.py` header). A crash after either call
     therefore leaves an incomplete run that is not resumed: no paid call is repeated and no outcome line is half-written.
   - **The writer** writes the parent's row, the yearly rows and their views in `open_thesis`, inside the article's commit, before
     judging. Its replay refuses an existing output folder, and an incomplete draw is never continued.
   - So a crash before the article's commit leaves none of these rows. A crash after it leaves the article's rows complete, in a run
     marked incomplete and not continued.
   - **The real-path proof** is a crash injected after the first yearly row's insert in `open_thesis`: the store then holds no row of
     that article. v2's "day atomicity" wording was wrong; it is article-level.
4. **The reading verdict is decisive.**
   - **Vetoes:** on any yearly ladder of T or M, a failing Q, O, R, L or N settled by both families (the gate's hard lines), a base-year
     question, or a lost qualification. On a C1 ladder, a hard-line fault that the frozen arm's ladders of that topic do not show.
   - **Both countings:** a lead must hold with unresolved points counted for the answer and counted against it. A lead under one only
     is inconclusive.
   - **Incomplete readings:** a missing third ruling, a missing first-reader row or a missed plant voids the reading (no verdict).
   - A refused yearly attempt counts in T's rule, not as a reading fault.
   - A control whose frozen sends disagree on kind is reported as **unexercised** for the kind rule; the case rule still applies to it.
5. **The spend bound:**
   - parent calls: 14 topics × 3 sends × 2 arms = 84;
   - yearly re-asks: up to 2 per new-version send that names the case, at most 84;
   - one retry per call.
   - That is at most about 336 calls at about $0.001 each, under a **ceiling of $0.40** on its own ledger, inside D439's remainder.

## 2. The boundary and every outcome

| Case | Who decides | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| One quantity, both years asked independently, both open | **the model:** typed case `one_quantity_two_periods` with `periods: [Y1, Y2]` | **code:** two distinct years, both this year or next by the step's existing year rules, so the case is accepted; the step is re-asked once per year with the one-line restriction |
| Either year ended, or beyond next year | — | **code:** the case is refused as malformed; re-asked once (the existing retry), then `refused twice`. Nothing is split |
| One open year as the question and another open year as its base or reference ("2027 against 2026") | **the model:** it must answer the asked year with the other as reference, as it does in the 20 stored one-year topics | **if the model names the case anyway, code cannot tell.** That is the misuse the probe's controls measure, and it is a stop condition |
| Two quantities, a second side (D436), a two-thing stake (Intel, Firmus) | the existing main-part rule | not this case; naming it without two open years is malformed (as above) |
| Several products over two open years | the model may name the case | each yearly re-ask meets the main-part rule for its year and may refuse |

**Every outcome of a split send:**
- both years write valid ladders, giving two views;
- one writes and the other refuses or fails validation twice, giving one view and the other year's refusal recorded on its row with its
  reason. This is not claimed equal to today's behaviour;
- both refuse or fail, giving no view and both refusals recorded;
- a re-ask naming the case again is malformed, and `refused twice` (no recursion).
- **Interrupted:** see §0b item 3. The boundary is article-level, with no resume.

**The schema change is a behaviour change, and it is measured as a new version.** `no_ladder` becomes `{case, reason, periods?}`, with
`case` a closed set: the existing prose decline cases, transcribed without a new rule, plus `one_quantity_two_periods`. The prompt
audit and a request diff come before any send.


